WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:26.280
I want you now to imagine a wearable robot that gives you superhuman abilities, or another

00:26.280 --> 00:34.000
one that takes wheelchair users up, standing and walking again.

00:34.000 --> 00:41.280
We at Berkeley Bionics call these robots exoskeletons.

00:41.280 --> 00:46.120
These are nothing else than something that you put on in the morning and it will give

00:46.120 --> 00:54.960
you extra strength and it will further enhance your speed and it will help you, for instance,

00:54.960 --> 00:56.520
to manage your balance.

00:56.520 --> 01:04.480
It is actually the true integration of the man and the machine, but not only that, it

01:04.480 --> 01:11.300
will integrate and network you to the universe and other devices out there.

01:11.300 --> 01:13.600
This is just not some blue sky thinking.

01:13.600 --> 01:20.360
To show you now what we are working on by starting out talking about the American soldier

01:20.360 --> 01:25.760
that on average does carry about 100 pounds on their backs and they are being asked to

01:25.760 --> 01:28.280
carry more equipment.

01:28.280 --> 01:36.620
Obviously this is resulting in some major complications, back injuries, 30% of them

01:36.620 --> 01:39.040
chronic back injuries.

01:39.040 --> 01:46.760
So we thought we would look at this challenge and create an exoskeleton that would help

01:46.760 --> 01:49.120
deal with this issue.

01:49.120 --> 01:56.640
So let me now introduce to you Herc, or the human universal load carrier.

01:56.640 --> 02:03.680
With the whole Texas Delta, I can carry 200 pounds over varied terrain for many hours.

02:03.680 --> 02:09.280
Its flexible design allows for deep squats, crawls, and high agility movements.

02:09.280 --> 02:14.920
It senses what I want to do, where I want to go, and then augments my strength and endurance.

02:14.920 --> 02:24.440
We are ready with our industry partner to introduce this device, this new exoskeleton

02:24.440 --> 02:25.600
this year.

02:25.600 --> 02:27.680
So it is for real.

02:27.680 --> 02:35.000
Now let's turn our heads towards the wheelchair users, something that I'm particularly passionate

02:35.000 --> 02:36.000
about.

02:36.000 --> 02:42.040
There are 68 million people estimated to be in wheelchairs worldwide.

02:42.080 --> 02:47.880
This is about 1% of the total population, and that's actually a conservative estimate.

02:47.880 --> 02:52.680
We are talking here about, oftentimes, very young individuals with spinal cord injuries

02:52.680 --> 02:58.560
that in the prime of the life, 20s, 30s, 40s hit the wall.

02:58.560 --> 03:00.200
And the wheelchair is the only option.

03:00.200 --> 03:06.780
But it's also the aging population that is multiplying in numbers, and the only option

03:06.780 --> 03:12.220
pretty much, whether it's stroke or other complications, is the wheelchair.

03:12.220 --> 03:17.820
And that is actually for the last 500 years, since it's a very successful introduction

03:17.820 --> 03:20.460
I must say.

03:20.460 --> 03:28.040
So we thought we would start writing a brand new chapter of mobility.

03:28.040 --> 03:36.400
Let me now introduce you to Elex, that is worn by Amanda Boxtel, that 19 years ago was

03:36.440 --> 03:38.760
a spinal cord injury.

03:38.760 --> 03:45.400
And as a result of that, she has not been able to walk for 19 years, until now.

03:45.400 --> 03:46.400
Thank you.

03:46.400 --> 04:05.000
Amanda is wearing our Elex like I said, it has sensors, it's completely non-invasive

04:05.040 --> 04:10.520
sensors in the crutches that send signals back to an on-board computer that is sitting

04:10.520 --> 04:16.800
here at her back, there are battery packs here as well that power motors that are sitting

04:16.800 --> 04:23.840
at her hips, as well as her knee joints that move her forward in this kind of smooth and

04:23.840 --> 04:28.600
very natural gait.

04:28.600 --> 04:32.840
I was 24 years old and at the top of my game.

04:32.840 --> 04:38.220
And a freak somersault while downhill skiing paralyzed me.

04:38.220 --> 04:45.960
In a split second, I lost all sensation and movement below my pelvis.

04:45.960 --> 04:53.480
Not long afterwards, a doctor strode into my hospital room and he said, Amanda, you'll

04:53.480 --> 04:57.000
never walk again.

04:57.000 --> 05:00.520
And that was 19 years ago.

05:00.520 --> 05:05.960
He robbed every ounce of hope from my being.

05:05.960 --> 05:12.760
Adaptive technology has since enabled me to learn how to downhill ski again, to rock climb

05:12.760 --> 05:25.760
and even hand cycle, but nothing has been invented that enables me to walk until now.

05:25.760 --> 05:43.400
As you can see, we have the technology, we have the platforms to sit down and have discussions

05:43.400 --> 05:45.640
with you.

05:45.640 --> 05:54.800
It's in our hands and we have all the potential here to change the life of future generations

05:54.800 --> 06:03.280
not only for the soldiers or for Amanda here and all the VLC users, but for everyone.

